			<div class="content" id="post-content-4836197">
									

<p>Reading this 4+ years after it came out, I wondered whether this or Good As New came out first. When I realized that it was the latter, I was disappointed that it appeared we were getting a retread of the same basic idea (although &quot;Eye points in the opposite direction of the host's natural eye&quot; becomes much funnier if you imagine it as polar opposite, rather than opposite direction within the socket).</p>
<p>When I got to the video log, I was pleasantly surprised to find that the overall plot of AR<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-270216-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-270216-1')">1</a></sup> being moved forward, as the seemingly invincible organization begins to crumble from within. The two letters, particularly Anderson's lack of a closing beyond a signature, were quite well done. It was impressive that none of the characters came across as one-dimensional, even within the constraints of a video log/letter. Since I can't be positive, I will say that the &quot;deal with the devil&quot; line felt a bit trite. Otherwise, though, outstanding work.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Vincent can always find another glorified accountant. But he only has one of me.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Pictured:</strong> Words that will not prove bitterly ironic at some point down the road.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-270216-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-270216-1')">1</a>. Still trying to figure out where I fall on the &quot;articles should stand in isolation&quot; scale, but I think that this sidesteps it by being relatively understandable by someone who might not have an idea about the broader Anderson Robotics series.</div>
</div>

